***********************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************************"},"output":false}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def checkValidString(self, s: str) -> bool:\n        lo = hi = 0  # min and max possible count of unmatched '(' so far\n        for ch in s:\n            if ch == '(':\n                lo += 1\n                hi += 1\n            elif ch == ')':\n                lo -= 1\n                hi -= 1\n            else:\n                lo -= 1  # '*' as ')'\n                hi += 1  # '*' as '('\n            if hi < 0:\n                return False  # too many ')' even if every '*' was '('\n            lo = max(lo, 0)  # never let the floor go negative: those '*' become empty\n        return lo == 0\n","approach":"**Instead of enumerating interpretations, track the set of possible open-bracket counts, which is always a contiguous range `[lo, hi]`.** A `'('` shifts the range up by 1, a `')'` shifts it down, and a `'*'` widens it by 1 on each side (it may be `'('`, `')'`, or nothing). Two checks keep it honest: if `hi < 0` even the most generous reading has an unmatched `')'`, so fail; and `lo` is clamped at 0 because a negative count is not a real state (the corresponding `'*'` are read as empty). The string is valid iff 0 is in the final range, i.e. `lo == 0`.\n\n**Why the range is contiguous:** every step maps an interval to an interval (shift or widen), and clamping at 0 keeps it an interval, so the two endpoints describe every achievable count exactly. This is the standard trick of compressing a DP over `(index, open_count)` into its reachable interval.\n\n**Alternatives to mention:** a two-pass greedy (left to right treating `*` as `(`, right to left treating `*` as `)`), or two stacks holding indices of `'('` and `'*'`, matching each `')'` with the nearest `'('` first and finally pairing leftover `'('` with `'*'` that come *after* them. The O(n^2) DP `valid[i][j]` is what you would reach for if the operators were more complicated.\n\n**Common mistakes:** returning `lo <= 0 <= hi` without clamping (then `lo` can go very negative and hide errors), forgetting the `hi < 0` early exit (fails on `\")(\"`), and backtracking over all 3^k choices.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Produce one concrete valid interpretation? (The two-stack method yields the assignments.) Count the number of valid interpretations? (DP on `(i, open)` with counts, O(n^2).) Multiple bracket types with wildcards? (Interval trick fails because type matters; stack-based DP.)","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1)"},"similar":["valid-parentheses","minimum-remove-to-make-valid-parentheses","longest-valid-parentheses"],"number":151,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def checkValidString(self, s: str) -> bool:\n        pass\n"}